ambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/prefill_alt_forced.log\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/prefill_cutoff1856_mixed.log\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/prefill_raw_fine.log\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/prefill_raw_focused.log\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/prefill_raw_forced.log\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/prefill_route_summary.csv\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/qkv_all80_r0.csv\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/qkv_all80_r0.log\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# CD3 startup patch backup\n\n- Generated: 2026-09-01 UTC\n- Source repository: `/root/projects/tiered-serve`\n- Source HEAD: `5d56a972bb80444671b9d27293e3fa18076c0d58`\n- Consolidated patch SHA-256: `95ad90192897b34d11f79f0cbc541552a61346cacd60d99b01bfdeb2fecf0c92`\n- Files changed: 27\n\n## Startup order\n\n1. `serving_max_batched_tokens.patch` (or its `from_1024` variant)\n2. `serving_performance_suite.patch`\n3. `apply_cd3.sh /root/projects/tiered-serve`\n\n`apply_cd3.sh` verifies and applies the consolidated `cd3_current.patch`.\nIt is idempotent. The old two-stage patch files are retained only as rollback\nartifacts and are not loaded at container startup.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis snapshot contains the current CD3 weight, fused-GEMM, KV codec, CPU\nweight/KV encoders, benchmark, serving integration, model configuration, and\nCD3 tests. Weight encode defaults to CPU; CUDA is used only to stage completed\ncompressed buffers and for decode/fused-MM. The legacy GPU encoder is available\nonly through the explicit debug override `TIERED_SERVE_CD3_WEIGHT_CPU_ENCODE=0`.\nUnrelated working-tree changes were intentionally excluded.\n\nThe CPU KV encoder additionally contains the 2026-09-01 optimization set:\n\n- block-owner, single-source-pass encoding;\n- min/max exception-free fast path;\n- SVE2 predicate counting on the exception path;\n- NEON hierarchical packing of 16 three-bit codes;\n- uninitialized temporary exception scratch.\n\nDirect-to-persistent Packed20 packing was measured but not retained because it\nwas 3–6% slower for medium and large batches.\n\nThe consolidated snapshot additionally contains:\n\n- host-native CD3 weight encoding with 64-row stripe parallelism;\n- adaptive OpenMP team sizing for a single KV compression worker;\n- batched NumPy KV slot allocation instead of a per-block Python loop;\n- production fused-MM crossover calibration through N=128 on GH200.\n- 128-thread standalone CD3 decode, with two logical chunks per thread;\n- two half-width live output accumulators with the same two 128-bit stores,\n  reducing standalone decode latency without changing the format;\n- 2-D `(tile, block)` KV decode launch geometry;\n- direct-to-cache indexed KV decode with no raw-BF16 intermediate copy;\n- selected-run KV staging for partial restores below the slot high-water mark.\n- one aligned slot-major CD3 blob and row-stride-aware CPU/GPU kernels;\n- one `cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` submission for all K/V staging runs;\n- cached block/slot device metadata and one native K/V decode dispatch.\n- active-set identity/content caching with generation-safe invalidation;\n- a dense steady-state decode plan that skips filtering, sorting and run scans.\n- a fully CPU P4Flat weight post-process (blocked relayout and subtile codec),\n  so CPU weight encoding no longer launches CUDA encode kernels;\n- a frozen-split weight benchmark that separately reports H2D, resident GPU\n  decode, and their combined latency.\n- projection-specific fused-N calibration overrides, with a uniform override\n  retained for compatibility;\n- exact-length CD3 frozen-split staging views, so standalone fallback accepts\n  variable-sized Packed20x3 exception streams;\n- startup route reporting that reflects projection-specific overrides.\n- a GH200/Llama-70B prefill weight-stream cutoff of N=1856: compressed\n  standalone decode below the cutoff and raw BF16 transfer at or above it;\n- an AST-level regression test that pins the calibrated CD3 cutoff.\n\n## Validation\n\n- The old CD3 patch's complete path set is contained in this patch.\n- Applied cleanly after startup prerequisites on source HEAD.\n- All 27 restored files matched the selected live files byte-for-byte.\n- `git diff --check`, Python byte compilation, and shell syntax checks passed.\n- The consolidated patch applied after both startup prerequisites in a clean\n  worktree and all 27 restored files matched the live files byte-for-byte.\n- 33 CD3, prefill weight-stream, and KV-prefill policy tests passed.\n- Actual Llama-3.1-70B weight round-trip: qkv/o/gateup/down all bit-exact.\n- Layer-40 CD3 CPU weight encode median total: 130.7 ms, versus P4Flat CPU\n  1951.9 ms (about 14.9x faster); compression ratio about 1.368x versus 1.326x.\n- GH200 CPU KV encode median with one serving worker, K+V: N=8 0.16 ms;\n  N=64 0.27 ms; N=128 0.37 ms; N=256 0.47 ms; N=1024 0.99 ms;\n  N=2048 2.02 ms. All cases were bit-exact.\n- Full serving initialization installed all 320 fused Linear overrides and\n  completed all 320 CPU-compressed weight cells.\n- Layer-40 standalone weight decode median gained another 1.4% in aggregate\n  from half-width live output accumulation (all four groups bit-exact).\n- Resident production KV decode improved 1.69x--2.04x for N=8/64/256 by\n  writing directly to the final cache. Partial H2D restore at high-water=1024\n  improved 16% for N=64 and 12% for N=256.\n- CPU-encoded production KV H2D+decode steady-state median on GH200 is\n  0.065/0.065/0.102/0.251 ms for N=8/64/256/1024. P4Flat under the same\n  split-measurement run is 0.063/0.070/0.107/0.302 ms. CD3 resident decode is\n  0.028/0.029/0.036/0.065 ms versus P4Flat 0.046/0.047/0.049/0.082 ms; CD3\n  H2D is 0.016/0.026/0.056/0.173 ms versus 0.030/0.042/0.081/0.232 ms.\n  The pre-blob CD3 values were\n  0.247/0.291/0.374/0.762 ms; all cases were bit-exact.\n- Layer-40 frozen-split weight medians on GH200 (qkv/o/gateup/down summed):\n  CD3 H2D 1.200 ms, resident decode 0.923 ms, H2D+decode 2.127 ms; P4Flat\n  H2D 1.353 ms, resident decode 0.906 ms, H2D+decode 2.232 ms. CD3 therefore\n  transfers 11.3% faster and finishes the production pair 4.7% faster while\n  its isolated decode is 1.9% slower. All four weights were bit-exact.\n- Rejected experiments are not present: warp-owner exception pre-application\n  was about 2.3x slower, pairwise 3xU32 code loading was neutral/regressive,\n  64-thread or register-forced 10-CTA kernels were 7--12% slower, and a\n  separate standalone exception kernel was 50.7% slower than integrated patch.\n- Fused route calibration with concurrent H2D found all four projections faster\n  for every tested N=65..128. At N=129 the Npad=192 transition makes fused\n  about 1.6--1.7x slower, so the production cutoff is exactly 128.\n- An all-80-layer QKV sweep found fused wins on 79/80 layers and saves\n  0.508/0.595/1.211 ms in aggregate at N=80/96/128. The exception-heavy layer\n  0 is the sole loser. End-to-end TPOT P50 for all-fused versus QKV-standalone\n  was 171.90/188.40/219.26 versus 172.20/187.96/219.30 ms at batch\n  80/96/128, confirming a tie at engine level and favoring all-fused because it\n  avoids dense-weight HBM writes.\n- End-to-end forced-route prefill sweeps placed the standalone/raw crossover\n  between N=1824 and N=1856. At N=1824 standalone led by 18.630 ms; at N=1856\n  raw led by 20.405 ms, and raw remained ahead through N=3072. An unforced run\n  confirmed standalone routing at N=1792/1824 and raw routing at N=1856/1888.\n  The full report and source logs are stored under\n  `/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/`.\n# CD3 optimization and P4Flat comparison\n\nDate: 2026-09-01 UTC\n\n## 1. Executive conclusion\n\nThe final CD3 implementation is not uniformly faster than P4Flat in every\nisolated operation. Its advantage comes from combining a denser lossless\nformat with substantially faster CPU encoding, less LPDDR-to-HBM traffic, and\na Hopper WGMMA fused path tuned for the decode batch sizes used by the serving\nworkload.\n\nOn the tested GH200 and Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct configuration:\n\n- Total compressed weight size is about 3.1% smaller: CD3 ratio is about\n  1.368x versus P4Flat 1.326x.\n- The LPDDR-resident portion transferred per layer in frozen-split mode is\n  376.586 MiB for CD3 versus 414.502 MiB for P4Flat, 9.15% fewer bytes.\n- CPU weight encoding of one layer's four fused projections is 14.9x to 28.1x\n  faster across the recorded runs. The latest median-of-five run was\n  128.830 ms versus 3620.297 ms.\n- Steady-state CPU KV encoding is 1.20x to 2.88x faster over N=8..2048.\n- Weight standalone H2D+decode is 4.36% faster overall. The H2D part is 10.89%\n  faster, while isolated resident decode is 1.45% slower.\n- In the latency-critical fused route, the sum of the four layer-40 projection\n  medians is 7.08% faster than P4Flat at N=64 and 14.62% faster at N=128 when\n  each codec uses its production route.\n- KV H2D-only and resident-decode-only are consistently faster in CD3, but the\n  complete production `decompress_batch` call is dispatch-dominated at small\n  N. Repeated fresh-process results range from 6--12% slower at N=8/64/256 to\n  statistical parity at N=1024. It is not valid to claim a robust KV\n  H2D+decode win at small N from the current measurements.\n\nThere is not yet a clean final-revision, apples-to-apples full serving\nTTFT/TPOT run of final CD3 against final P4Flat. The percentages above are\ncomponent and per-layer route measurements. Older 2026-08-27 serving results\npredate the final CD3 kernels and routing, so they are intentionally excluded\nfrom the headline comparison.\n\n## 2. Test platform and terminology\n\n- GPU/system: NVIDIA GH200, compute capability 9.0, Grace ARM CPU, CUDA 13.2.\n- Model weights: real `meta-llama/Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct`, TP=1, BF16.\n- Representative layer: transformer layer 40 unless otherwise stated.\n- Fused serving weights:\n  - QKV: 10240 x 8192, 160 MiB raw BF16.\n  - O: 8192 x 8192, 128 MiB.\n  - GateUp: 57344 x 8192, 896 MiB.\n  - Down: 8192 x 28672, 448 MiB.\n  - Total per layer: 1632 MiB (1.59375 GiB).\n- `N` is the flattened activation row/token count presented to a linear layer.\n- All retained codec paths are lossless; reported comparisons passed raw BF16\n  bit-exact checks.\n\nThe measurements use the following distinct definitions:\n\n- CPU encode: BF16 input is on the CPU. Time includes production codec CPU\n  work and synchronization, but never a CUDA encode kernel.\n- Resident decode: compressed data is already in GPU HBM. Only the GPU decode\n  kernel is timed.\n- H2D-only: only the LPDDR/pinned-host buffers required for the selected path\n  are copied into preallocated HBM staging. On GH200 this is Grace LPDDR to GPU\n  HBM over NVLink-C2C, not a conventional discrete-GPU PCIe transfer.\n- H2D+decode: the production transfer and decode operation is timed. It is an\n  independent measurement, not necessarily the arithmetic sum of separately\n  measured H2D and decode medians.\n- Fused: compressed weight reconstruction occurs in the register-A WGMMA\n  kernel and no dense BF16 weight is emitted to HBM.\n- Standalone: the full BF16 weight is reconstructed in HBM and then consumed\n  by a separate GEMM.\n- Raw: the full uncompressed BF16 weight is transferred and no codec decode\n  kernel runs.\n\n## 3. Format and memory footprint\n\nCD3 preserves the BF16 sign and mantissa byte verbatim and encodes the exponent\nas a 3-bit delta from one base exponent per 16 values. A 64x64 tile therefore\ncontains 1536 code bytes, 256 base bytes, and a sparse exception stream.\nExceptions use a 20-bit `(12-bit tile position, 8-bit exponent)` entry, packed\nthree entries into each 64-bit word (`Packed20x3`).\n\nCodes 0 through 7 are all normal CD3 deltas. An exception temporarily carries\ncode 0 and is distinguished by its Packed20 position/mask. Consequently code 7\ncannot be used as an exception sentinel without changing the format. The\nEscape7 experiment increased exceptions by 85.6%, slowed decode by 11.11%, and\nwould reduce the expected KV compression ratio to about 1.3347x, close to\nP4Flat's 1.3259x.\n\nFor representative layer-40 weights:\n\n| Metric | CD3 | P4Flat | CD3 change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Total compression ratio | 1.368x | 1.326x | 3.1% fewer compressed bytes |\n| Frozen-split LPDDR transfer/layer | 376.586 MiB | 414.502 MiB | 9.15% fewer bytes |\n\nBoth codecs retain sign/mantissa in HBM for frozen-split weights. CD3 also\nretains tile exception offsets in HBM. The table includes all transferred\nmetadata, so auxiliary structures are accounted for in the comparison.\n\nKV is different: offloaded KV does not retain the sign/mantissa half in HBM.\nThe selected K and V compressed slot blobs are transferred in full and decoded\ndirectly into the final GPU KV cache.\n\n## 4. CPU encoding\n\n### 4.1 Weight CPU encode\n\nInput was the four real layer-40 fused BF16 CPU tensors listed above. Each\ncodec ran in a fresh process on NUMA node 0, with one untimed warm-up and five\ntimed repetitions per projection; the table contains medians. No GPU encoder\nwas called.\n\n| Projection | CD3 (ms) | P4Flat (ms) | Speedup |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| QKV | 15.824 | 354.944 | 22.4x |\n| O | 12.710 | 285.589 | 22.5x |\n| GateUp | 79.161 | 1987.135 | 25.1x |\n| Down | 21.135 | 992.629 | 47.0x |\n| Sum of medians | 128.830 | 3620.297 | 28.1x |\n\nAn earlier recorded run was 130.7 ms versus 1951.9 ms (14.9x). CD3 is stable\naround 129--131 ms, while P4Flat CPU time is sensitive to host/OpenMP state.\nThe defensible conclusion is an order-of-magnitude win, observed between\n14.9x and 28.1x, rather than one universal multiplier.\n\nWeight encode is normally an offline/load-time step, so this gain mostly\nreduces initialization/preprocessing time and prevents codec preparation from\nstealing GPU SM cycles; it is not added to token latency.\n\n### 4.2 KV CPU encode\n\nInput was real cached K and V blocks from\n`/root/projects/ats-benchmarks/benchmarks/compress/kv_blocks_cache.pt`, each\nblock shaped `[16,8,128]` BF16. K+V is 64 KiB per block. The production\n`compress_batch_async_from_host` plus `wait_compress_async` path was measured\nwith one compression worker, `OMP=all`, NUMA node 0, one warm-up and 20 timed\niterations. GPU decode was used only after timing to verify bit exactness.\n\n| Blocks N | K+V input | CD3 median | P4Flat median | CD3 speedup |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 8 | 0.5 MiB | 0.17 ms | 0.49 ms | 2.88x |\n| 64 | 4 MiB | 0.28 ms | 0.55 ms | 1.96x |\n| 128 | 8 MiB | 0.40 ms | 0.60 ms | 1.50x |\n| 256 | 16 MiB | 0.49 ms | 0.68 ms | 1.39x |\n| 1024 | 64 MiB | 1.04 ms | 1.25 ms | 1.20x |\n| 2048 | 128 MiB | 1.99 ms | 2.48 ms | 1.25x |\n\n## 5. Weight standalone H2D/decode comparison\n\nThis used `benchmarks/weight_cpu_h2d_decode.py` with the real layer-40 fused\nweights, CPU encoding for both codecs, 10 warm-ups and 50 samples. Encode and\nsetup were outside CUDA timing. Sign/mantissa stayed in HBM; only the\nLPDDR-side exponent representation was transferred using the same\n`cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` helper for both codecs.\n\n| Sum across QKV/O/GateUp/Down | CD3 | P4Flat | CD3 change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| H2D-only | 1.2027 ms | 1.3497 ms | 10.89% faster |\n| Resident decode-only | 0.9211 ms | 0.9079 ms | 1.45% slower |\n| Serialized H2D+decode | 2.1286 ms | 2.2256 ms | 4.36% faster |\n\nThus the weight win is a transfer win, not a pure decoder win. CD3's 3-bit\ncodes reduce the LPDDR traffic enough to compensate for a slightly more\nexpensive decoder. Per-projection H2D+decode medians were also all lower for\nCD3: QKV 0.2183 vs 0.2338 ms, O 0.1808 vs 0.1904 ms, GateUp 1.1430 vs\n1.1920 ms, and Down 0.5864 vs 0.6095 ms.\n\n## 6. KV H2D/decode comparison\n\nThe input was the same real K/V block cache as the CPU encode test. Each codec\nand block count ran in a new process with CPU encoding, one worker, 20 warm-ups\nand 100 CUDA-event samples. Three complete rounds were run; the table reports\nthe median of the three per-process medians. No GPU encoder was called.\n\n| N | CD3 H2D | P4 H2D | CD3 resident decode | P4 resident decode | CD3 production total | P4 production total |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 8 | 0.0171 | 0.0289 | 0.0309 | 0.0458 | 0.0703 | 0.0629 |\n| 64 | 0.0260 | 0.0376 | 0.0287 | 0.0448 | 0.0686 | 0.0647 |\n| 256 | 0.0559 | 0.0666 | 0.0371 | 0.0466 | 0.1004 | 0.0915 |\n| 1024 | 0.1730 | 0.1849 | 0.0668 | 0.0736 | 0.2486 | 0.2497 |\n\nAll values are milliseconds. CD3 reduces H2D-only by 40.8%, 30.9%, 16.1%,\nand 6.4%, and resident decode by 32.5%, 35.9%, 20.4%, and 9.2% at the four\nsizes. However, the complete production call is 11.8%, 6.0%, and 9.7% slower\nat N=8/64/256 and only 0.4% faster at N=1024. At these sub-0.1-ms sizes,\nPython/native dispatch, plan lookup, stream/event handling, and K/V submission\noverhead dominate the kernels that were optimized.\n\nAn earlier single measurement recorded a more favorable CD3 production total\n(0.065/0.065/0.102/0.251 ms versus P4Flat 0.063/0.070/0.107/0.302 ms). The\nfresh three-round result above supersedes that single run for conclusions: the\nseparate H2D and decode improvements are robust, while small-N production\ntotal ordering is not.\n\n## 7. Fused decode+GEMM comparison\n\nInput weights were the four real layer-40 projections. Activations were seeded\nrandom BF16 `[N,K]` tensors. Sign/mantissa stayed resident in HBM and the\nLPDDR-side metadata was copied before the compute stream ran. L2 was flushed\nfor every sample; results are P50 after 10 warm-ups and 50 iterations. The\nnumbers below sum the four independently measured projection medians, so they\nare a per-layer component estimate, not an end-to-end TPOT result.\n\nCodec encode was setup only and excluded from timing. CD3 setup used its\ndefault CPU weight encoder; the P4Flat benchmark helper prepared its object\nwith the existing GPU encoder. This does not affect the timed H2D/kernel\ncomparison because both produce the production bit-exact format, but it must\nnot be read as an encode comparison.\n\n| N | P4Flat production route | CD3 production route | P4 total | CD3 total | CD3 gain |\n|---:|:---|:---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 32 | all fused | all fused | 2.2400 ms | 2.0096 ms | 10.28% |\n| 48 | all fused | all fused | 2.2539 ms | 2.0574 ms | 8.72% |\n| 64 | all fused | all fused | 2.2644 ms | 2.1041 ms | 7.08% |\n| 128 | QKV standalone, other three fused | all fused | 2.6661 ms | 2.2762 ms | 14.62% |\n\nAt N=128, forcing P4Flat QKV to remain fused gives 2.8080 ms versus CD3\n2.2756 ms, a 18.96% total reduction. The forced fused-kernel-only sums are\n1.5953 ms versus 1.1727 ms, a 26.49% reduction. This forced result is useful\nfor comparing kernels, but the 14.62% production-policy result is the fair\nserving route comparison.\n\nRelative to the early CD3 fused baseline discussed during development:\n\n- At N=64, CD3 kernel sum moved from 1.322 ms to 0.996 ms, about 24.7% lower.\n  It changed from 22.9% slower than P4Flat (1.076 ms) to 5.1% faster than the\n  current P4Flat kernel sum (1.050 ms).\n- At N=128, CD3 moved from 1.573 ms to 1.173 ms, about 25.4% lower. Its\n  forced-fused lead over P4Flat grew from about 4.6% to 26.5%.\n\n## 8. Production route policy\n\nThe final CD3 policy for the tested model/configuration is:\n\n- N <= 128: all QKV/O/GateUp/Down use fused decode+WGMMA.\n- Eager prefill N=129..1855: transfer compressed metadata, standalone-decode\n  the weight, then run GEMM.\n- Eager prefill N >= 1856: transfer raw BF16 and skip codec decode.\n\nCurrent P4Flat uses QKV fused only through N=64 and O/GateUp/Down through\nN=128. Its frozen-split standalone/raw prefill cutoff is 1330.\n\nThe CD3 standalone/raw cutoff was measured end-to-end with Llama-70B, not\nderived only from a bandwidth model. Five runs were made at each point and the\nfirst-use sample was discarded. At N=1824, standalone was 470.965 ms versus\n489.595 ms raw; at N=1856, raw was 459.620 ms versus 480.025 ms standalone.\nRaw remained ahead through N=3072. An unforced run confirmed the route switch\nexactly at 1856.\n\nThe 1856 versus 1330 cutoff should not be presented as a 39.5% direct\nperformance speedup: it says that CD3 remains worth decoding over a 526-token\nwider range under this residency/fabric/model combination.\n\nAn all-80-layer CD3 QKV sweep found fused faster on 79/80 layers and saved\n0.508/0.595/1.211 ms in aggregate at N=80/96/128 versus QKV standalone. Layer\n0 was the exception-heavy outlier. At full engine level, all-CD3-fused versus\nCD3-QKV-standalone TPOT P50 was 171.90/188.40/219.26 ms versus\n172.20/187.96/219.30 ms for batch 80/96/128: effectively tied because other\nserving work masks the per-layer difference. This is a CD3 route ablation, not\na CD3-versus-P4Flat end-to-end result.\n\n## 9. Retained optimization work\n\n### 9.1 CPU encoder\n\n- Block-owner one-pass encoding: one OpenMP owner completes a KV block in\n  tile/thread/item order, eliminating global prefix buffers, barriers, and the\n  second source-exponent read.\n- Min/max exception-free fast path: if 16 exponents fit in one 3-bit range,\n  candidate search and per-element exception work are skipped.\n- Direct local Packed20x3 construction with uninitialized temporary exception\n  scratch; only valid entries are compacted.\n- NEON extraction of exponent/sign-mantissa and hierarchical packing of 16\n  three-bit codes.\n- SVE2 predicate counting only on the uncommon exception path.\n- CPU weight encoding by independent 64-row stripes, preserving the exact GPU\n  tile order while exposing enough parallel work.\n- Adaptive OpenMP team sizing and worker affinity, especially for an explicit\n  single KV worker.\n- Batched NumPy KV slot assignment instead of a per-block Python loop.\n- Weight encode defaults to CPU. GPU encode remains only behind the explicit\n  debug override `TIERED_SERVE_CD3_WEIGHT_CPU_ENCODE=0`.\n\n### 9.2 Standalone GPU decoder\n\n- Aligned two-`uint32` loading of each six-byte/48-bit code chunk. The final\n  thread reads bytes 1528..1535, so it never crosses a 1536-byte tile.\n- 128 physical threads per tile, each handling two logical 16-value chunks,\n  improving occupancy relative to the original 256-thread layout.\n- Two half-width live output accumulators followed by the same two 128-bit\n  stores, reducing register pressure. This improved aggregate layer-40 weight\n  decode by about 1.4%.\n- 2-D `(tile, block)` KV launch geometry.\n- Direct-to-final-cache indexed KV decode, eliminating the intermediate raw\n  BF16 copy.\n\n### 9.3 KV transport and orchestration\n\n- One aligned slot-major blob containing codes, sign/mantissa, bases, offsets,\n  and Packed20x3 entries, with explicit row strides in CPU/GPU kernels.\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/CD3_OPTIMIZATION_AND_P4FLAT_COMPARISON.md\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/PREFILL_ROUTE_REPORT.md\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/e2e_all128/20260901_154926/manifest.txt\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/e2e_all128/20260901_154926/round-01_cao-cd3-fused.log\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/e2e_all128/20260901_154926/summary.tsv\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/e2e_direct/all128.csv\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/e2e_direct/all128.log\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/e2e_direct/qkv64.log\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/e2e_direct/qkv64_retry.csv\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/e2e_direct/qkv64_retry.log\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/edge_128_r0.csv\n/lambda/nfs/whcho